# The sample report embeds a byte-exact copy of the built .pbiviz, and
# verify-package compares those files against the archive it just produced.
# Line-ending conversion would break that comparison on some checkouts.
samples/**/CustomVisuals/** -text

# Power BI writes the project definition as UTF-8; keep it stable across
# platforms so regenerating the sample never shows a whitespace-only diff.
samples/**/*.json text eol=lf
samples/**/*.tmdl text eol=lf
samples/**/*.pbip text eol=lf
samples/**/*.pbir text eol=lf
samples/**/*.pbism text eol=lf

# Text assets that pbiviz embeds into the package. core.autocrlf gives Windows
# a CRLF working copy of files the repository stores as LF, so any asset that
# reaches the package verbatim rather than being parsed and re-serialised would
# hash differently on Windows and Linux from the very same commit. Pinning them
# to LF removes that dependency.
#
# This repository is not currently affected - a Windows build and the Linux CI
# runner produce the same SHA-256 for the same commit - because pbiviz parses
# these files rather than copying them. That is a property of the tool, not a
# guarantee, and npm run verify-reproducible-package cannot detect a regression
# because it only compares two builds on one machine.
*.resjson text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.less text eol=lf

# Written by npm run screenshots and read back by the publication gate. Without
# this pin a Windows checkout converts it to CRLF and the next capture rewrites
# it as LF, so the file shows a whole-file diff that has nothing to do with the
# screenshots it records.
assets/screenshot-capture.json text eol=lf

